  "transcriptText": "Welcome to other tables. Top pair against one player is strong. Against three? A♦ J♣, on J♥ 7♠ 3♦. How often is someone already ahead... against one, two, or three? Take a guess.\n\nLet's count, against random hands, just as an illustration. One player... 43 of his 1,081 hands are ahead. 4.0%. A second... 7.8%. A third... 11.5%. No future cards. Each seat just adds hands that already beat you.\n\nNow bluff. 2/3 of the pot needs 40% folds. Say each player folds 60%, on his own. Heads-up, that's enough. Against two, both must fold: 0.6 × 0.6, 36%. Against three, 21.6%. The bluff that works against one fails against two.\n\nYour turn. Two players, each folds 70%. Both fold? 0.7 × 0.7... 49%. Above 40%, so you can bet. Every extra player is another range to beat. Bluff less, and value bet stronger hands."
